1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,502
Hi, this is Tanmay Kulkarni,
in for Humanitarians AI.

2
00:00:03,928 --> 00:00:06,240
In this puzzle, a coin flip
beats a learning algorithm

3
00:00:06,260 --> 00:00:08,378
by more than three and a half times.

4
00:00:08,529 --> 00:00:11,235
Here's why, and what it
says about randomness.

5
00:00:11,800 --> 00:00:14,778
It's from Sutton and Barto's
reinforcement learning textbook.

6
00:00:14,818 --> 00:00:17,367
Four squares. The second
one swaps your buttons.

7
00:00:17,407 --> 00:00:20,199
You can't see which square you're on,
and you can't count your steps.

8
00:00:20,239 --> 00:00:22,827
So whatever you press,
you press everywhere.

9
00:00:23,183 --> 00:00:26,523
A learning algorithm that hunts for the
best button settles on mostly right,

10
00:00:26,543 --> 00:00:30,249
and bounces off that swapped
square for about forty-four steps.

11
00:00:30,695 --> 00:00:32,401
There is no best button here.

12
00:00:32,900 --> 00:00:34,942
A fair coin gets there
in about twelve steps.

13
00:00:34,982 --> 00:00:36,982
And the best coin isn't even fair.

14
00:00:37,181 --> 00:00:42,239
Bias it to land right about 58.6%
of the time: 2 − √2, exactly.

15
00:00:42,604 --> 00:00:44,759
That's what chapter 13
of the book is about:

16
00:00:44,799 --> 00:00:48,702
learners that tune the coin itself,
and find that number on their own.

17
00:00:48,742 --> 00:00:51,792
So why random? Give the player
more information and find out.

18
00:00:51,832 --> 00:00:55,992
Let it see where it is, and the coin
vanishes: right, left, right, three steps.

19
00:00:56,032 --> 00:00:57,602
But in rock, paper, scissors,

20
00:00:57,622 --> 00:01:00,435
against an opponent who learns
your habits, the coin survives.

21
00:01:00,475 --> 00:01:02,533
Lean on rock, and you get punished.

22
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:04,560
Next time the best move is random,

23
00:01:04,580 --> 00:01:07,199
ask what the player can't
see, or who's watching.

24
00:01:07,239 --> 00:01:10,383
Try it on a goalkeeper at a penalty,
choosing which way to dive.

25
00:01:10,423 --> 00:01:13,277
Does more information make the
randomness vanish, or survive?

26
00:01:13,317 --> 00:01:16,167
Tell me in the comments, and
name the clue or the watcher.

27
00:01:16,207 --> 00:01:19,501
Saying it's random because
it's uncertain doesn't count.

28
00:01:19,758 --> 00:01:22,272
The full video works
through it, step by step.

29
00:01:22,312 --> 00:01:25,176
Tanmay Kulkarni, in for Humanitarians AI.
